# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Why `pubspec.lock` IS committed (and `ios/Podfile.lock` too)
#
# The default `flutter create` .gitignore excludes both, because most published
# *packages* want to float their dependencies. This repository is an
# *application*, and the rule is the opposite:
#
#   * `pubspec.lock` pins the exact resolved version of every dependency. If it
#     is not committed, two developers on the same commit can get different
#     builds of `cloud_firestore` — and a crash that reproduces for one person
#     and not another is a bug that survives a week of CI.
#   * `Podfile.lock` does the same for the iOS side. It is committed for the
#     same reason, despite the Flutter template's default.
#
# What must never be committed is signing material (`*.jks`, `*.keystore`,
# `*.mobileprovision`, `key.properties`), which is covered above.
